  "generated_text": "To solve for the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we need to analyze the given properties of the trapezoid \\(ABCD\\).\n\n1. **Identify Key Properties:**\n   - \\(AD \\parallel BC\\), so \\(ABCD\\) is a trapezoid.\n   - \\(\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}\\), so \\(\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - \\(\\overline{AC}\\) bisects \\(\\angle BAD\\), so \\(\\angle BAC = \\angle CAD\\).\n\n2. **Determine the Geometry:**\n   Since \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is the angle bisector of \\(\\angle BAD\\) and \\(\\overline{AC} \\perp \\overline{CD}\\), \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is a right triangle with \\(\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ\\). This means that \\(\\angle CAD = \\angle BAC\\), and since \\(\\overline{AC}\\) is the angle bisector, \\(\\angle CAD = \\angle BAC = \\theta\\).\n\n3. **Use the Area of Trapezoid:**\n   The area of trapezoid \\(ABCD\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   [ABCD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (AD + BC) \\times h\n   \\]\n   where \\(h\\) is the height of the trapezoid, which is the length of \\(\\overline{AC}\\). Let's denote the height \\(h = AC = x\\).\n\n4. **Area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\):**\n   The area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is:\n   \\[\n   [ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AD \\times h = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AD \\times x\n   \\]\n   We need to find the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) in terms of the given area of the trapezoid. Notice that the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is half the area of the trapezoid because the height \\(x\\) is the same as the height of the trapezoid and the base \\(AD\\) is the average of the bases of the trapezoid.\n\n5. **Calculate the Area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\):**\n   Since the area of the trapezoid is 42, the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is half of this:\n   \\[\n   [ACD] = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 42 = 21\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).",
